The problem asks for the minimum time required to brew $m$ potions by $n$ wizards sequentially.
- Each potion $j$ has mana capacity $mana[j]$.
- Each wizard $i$ has skill level $skill[i]$.
- Time taken by wizard $i$ on potion $j$ is $time_{ij} = skill[i] \times mana[j]$.
- A potion must be passed to the next wizard immediately. This means if potion $j$ starts at time $S_j$, then:
  - Wizard 0 finishes it at $F_{0,j} = S_j + skill[0] \times mana[j]$
  - Wizard 1 starts it at $F_{0,j}$ and finishes it at $F_{1,j} = F_{0,j} + skill[1] \times mana[j]$
  - ...
  - Wizard $i$ finishes it at $F_{i,j} = S_j + \sum_{k=0}^i (skill[k] \times mana[j])$

The constraints are:
1. A wizard can only work on one potion at a time. For any two potions $j$ and $j+1$:
   - Wizard $i$ finishes potion $j$ before starting potion $j+1$.
   - $F_{i,j} \le S_{j+1}$ (Wait, this is not quite right. The start time of potion $j+1$ at wizard $i$ must be $\ge F_{i,j}$).
