     $S_{j+1} = S_j + skill[0] \times mana[j]$ is NOT necessarily true.
     The start time of potion $j$ at wizard $k$ is $T_{k,j}$.
     $T_{k,j} = T_{k-1,j} + skill[k-1] \times mana[j]$ for $k > 0$.
     $T_{0,j}$ is the start time of potion $j$ at wizard 0.
     The condition "passed immediately" means:
     $T_{k,j+1} = T_{k,j} + skill[k] \times mana[j]$ for all $k$.
     Wait, let's re-read: "a potion must be passed to the next wizard immediately after the current wizard completes their work."
     This means for a fixed potion $j$:
     $T_{0,j}$ = start time at wizard 0.
     $T_{1,j} = T_{0,j} + skill[0] \times mana[j]$
     $T_{2,j} = T_{1,j} + skill[1] \times mana[j]$
     ...
     $T_{i,j} = T_{i-1,j} + skill[i-1] \times mana[j]$
     And for a fixed wizard $i$:
     Wizard $i$ works on potion $j$ during $[T_{i,j}, T_{i,j} + skill[i] \times mana[j]]$.
     The next potion $j+1$ must start at wizard $i$ only after wizard $i$ finishes potion $j$:
     $T_{i,j+1} \ge T_{i,j} + skill[i] \times mana[j]$.

